Troubleshooting
Troubleshooting
Start here when:

R cannot find a file;
a package will not load;
Quarto will not render;
an object cannot be found;
your code works in the Console but not when rendering; or
you are unsure how to interpret an error message.

1. Inspect and prepare
Identify:

the observational unit;
the grouping variable;
the numerical response variable;
the sample size in each context.

In one or two sentences, state what archaeological comparison these observations can reasonably inform.
Remember that these archaeological specimens are not automatically a random probability sample of all Teotihuacan leporids.

7. Interpret the p-value
Report the p-value as a continuous value.
Explain it as a tail probability conditional on the null model.
Your interpretation must make clear that the p-value is not:

the probability that the null hypothesis is true;
the probability that leporid management occurred;
the probability that chance caused the result;
the magnitude of the isotope difference; or
a measure of archaeological importance.

Final interpretation
Answer the research question in complete sentences.
Your conclusion should integrate:

effect magnitude;
uncertainty;
the null-model comparison;
archaeological significance;
limitations.

Do not end with only “reject H0” or “statistically significant.”

Estimate first. Quantify uncertainty. Test a clearly defined null model when useful. Then return to archaeology.

Assignment
Using data/seshat_missingness_counts.csv, conduct a descriptive sensitivity analysis of the missing-data decision at the center of the Seshat moralizing-gods debate.
You will:

inspect the teaching dataset and confirm what one row represents;
calculate counts and proportions for the three documented outcome statuses;
visualize the data while preserving missing_unknown as its own category;
construct an alternative summary in which missing_unknown is combined with known_absent;
compare the two representations numerically and visually; and
explain what the comparison shows about the consequences of treating unknown information as documented absence.

The assignment is about the effect of an analytical decision, not about deciding whether moralizing gods actually were present in the unknown cases.

4. Construct the alternative unknown → absent representation
Now reproduce the logic of the disputed coding decision at the aggregate level.
Do not overwrite dat. Create a new object.

recoded &lt;- dat |&gt;
  mutate(
    recoded_status = case_when(
      status == "known_present" ~ "present",
      status %in% c("known_absent", "missing_unknown") ~ "absent"
    )
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(recoded_status) |&gt;
  summarize(
    count = sum(count),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(
    proportion = count / sum(count),
    percent = 100 * proportion
  )

recoded

# A tibble: 2 × 4
  recoded_status count proportion percent
  &lt;chr&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;      &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;
1 absent           502      0.627    62.7
2 present          299      0.373    37.3


Verify that the total number of underlying observations remains 801.

recoded |&gt;
  summarize(total_observations = sum(count))

# A tibble: 1 × 1
  total_observations
               &lt;dbl&gt;
1                801


Then report:

the number and percentage classified as present;
the number and percentage classified as absent after recoding; and
how many observations changed interpretive status because of this decision.

Be precise: the 490 underlying observations did not acquire new historical evidence. Their analytical label changed.

Submission
Submit both:

your completed Quarto source file (.qmd); and
the rendered HTML file (.html).

Your report should include:

inspection of the teaching data;
the three-category count/proportion summary;
a visualization preserving unknown outcomes;
the alternative unknown → absent summary;
a direct numerical comparison;
a visualization comparing the two representations; and
a concise final interpretation.

Keep the analysis reproducible. Do not manually type calculated percentages into the report when R can calculate them from the teaching file.
Before submitting, restart R and render the complete document from a clean session. Confirm that all code executes and all figures and tables appear correctly.

2. Calculate descriptive statistics
For d13c and d15n, separately for La Bastida and Gatas, calculate:

mean;
median;
standard deviation (SD);
interquartile range (IQR);
minimum;
maximum; and
range.

Present the results clearly so that the two sites can be compared.

# Your code

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish populations, samples, parameters, statistics, and estimates;
explain sampling variability and why estimates require measures of uncertainty;
distinguish point estimates from interval estimates;
interpret a 95% confidence interval appropriately;
explain bootstrap confidence intervals;
distinguish bootstrap distributions from permutation null distributions;
state null and alternative hypotheses;
explain a p-value as a tail probability under a specified null model;
distinguish statistical significance, effect magnitude, uncertainty, and archaeological significance;
identify limits created by sampling, non-independence, exchangeability, measurement, chronology, and comparison design; and
trace the inferential chain in the Teotihuacan leporid case.

4. Bootstrap Confidence Intervals
The bootstrap approximates uncertainty by resampling:

start with the observed sample of size \(n\);
draw \(n\) observations with replacement;
calculate the statistic;
store the estimate;
repeat many times;
examine the bootstrap distribution; and
use that distribution to quantify uncertainty.

A simple percentile 95% interval uses approximately the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap estimates.

Bootstrap: How much might my estimate vary?

The bootstrap distribution is a distribution of repeatedly calculated statistics, not individual specimens.
Bootstrapping cannot repair biased preservation, recovery, context selection, laboratory selection, a mismatch between sample and target population, or non-independence. Ten thousand resamples still contain only the information supplied by the original data.

17. Count and visualize the three states
Your first descriptive task is to distinguish present, absent, and unknown. Do not silently drop NA from this audit.

seshat |&gt;
  mutate(status = case_when(
    is.na(moralizing_gods) ~ "Unknown",
    moralizing_gods == 1 ~ "Present",
    moralizing_gods == 0 ~ "Absent"
  )) |&gt;
  count(status) |&gt;
  mutate(proportion = n / sum(n))

Use clear labels in the visualization so unknown cases cannot be mistaken for absences.

Learning objectives
By the end of Week 4, you should be able to:

explain the purpose of descriptive statistics;
distinguish a population parameter from a sample statistic;
identify the unit of analysis before calculating a statistic;
connect nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio measurement to defensible summaries;
explain why numerical coding does not turn a nominal variable into a quantitative one;
define and interpret the mode, median, arithmetic mean, and trimmed mean;
explain why the mean is a balance point and why it is sensitive to extreme values;
identify unimodal, bimodal/multimodal, symmetric, right-skewed, and left-skewed distributions;
define and interpret the range, quartiles, IQR, variance, standard deviation, MAD, and coefficient of variation;
explain why range is fragile and why IQR is resistant;
distinguish population variance/SD from sample variance/SD;
explain why sample variance and sample SD use (n-1);
interpret standard deviation in the original measurement units;
apply the 68–95 empirical rule only when an approximately normal distribution makes it appropriate;
explain why median + IQR and mean + SD are complementary descriptive pairs;
read a histogram and boxplot as representations of distribution shape and spread;
treat boxplot “outliers” as observations requiring investigation rather than automatic deletion;
explain why CV is a relative measure of variability suitable for ratio-scale measurements with a meaningful nonzero mean;
explain why CV should not be used for Assignment 1’s delta isotope values;
use R to inspect, reshape, summarize, and visualize the Assignment 1 data;
calculate mean, median, SD, IQR, minimum, maximum, and range separately by site and isotope;
compare mean with median and SD with IQR when evaluating a distribution;
write a concise descriptive interpretation that separates observed patterns from causal explanation; and
render and submit a reproducible Quarto analysis containing both code and interpretation.

Descriptive versus inferential statistics
Descriptive statistics summarize the observations actually present in a dataset.
Examples include:

counts and proportions;
mean and median;
range and IQR;
variance and standard deviation;
histograms and boxplots.

Inferential statistics use sample information to make uncertainty-aware statements about a larger population or data-generating process.
Assignment 1 is explicitly a descriptive assignment. No inferential statistical test is required (Knipper et al. 2020).






WarningThe Assignment 1 archaeological sample is not the whole El Argar population



The teaching file contains 69 evaluable human observations: 48 from La Bastida and 21 from Gatas.
Those people are an archaeological sample preserved, excavated, sampled, and successfully analyzed under particular conditions. Describing these observations does not by itself justify population-wide claims about every person who lived at either settlement—or about El Argar society as a whole.

Boxplots: a compact display of center and spread
A conventional Tukey boxplot shows:

lower edge of box = (Q_1);
line inside box = median;
upper edge of box = (Q_3);
box width in the measurement dimension = IQR;
whiskers extending to observations within the conventional 1.5-IQR limits;
observations beyond the whiskers plotted individually.







ImportantA boxplot ‘outlier’ is not automatically an error



The 1.5-IQR rule identifies observations that are unusual relative to the distribution.
It does not tell you why they are unusual.
An unusual archaeological value may represent:

a recording error;
a laboratory problem;
a different age or biological condition;
a real minority behavior;
chronological mixing;
a distinct social group; or
an entirely legitimate extreme case.

Inspect the observation and its context before deciding what it means.

Variation can itself be evidence
Dispersion is not merely a technical statistic added after the mean.
Variation can be the phenomenon of interest.
In biological anthropology, variation may bear on:

sexual dimorphism;
taxonomic heterogeneity;
geographic variation;
developmental plasticity; or
environmental effects.

Plavcan’s early-Homo analysis treats size variation as evidence that must be interpreted comparatively and cautiously rather than as noise to be removed (Plavcan 2012).

What δ13C and δ15N can tell us
Stable carbon and nitrogen isotope values are useful for reconstructing aspects of food webs and diet.
In broad terms:

(^{13}) reflects carbon sources and ecological inputs into the food web;
(^{15}) generally increases across trophic levels and is often related to dietary protein sources.

But Knipper et al. stress that environmental and agricultural processes also influence isotope baselines (Knipper et al. 2020).
For example:

plant water conditions can affect carbon-isotope values;
manuring can raise crop (^{15});
animal feeding practices affect faunal isotope baselines;
breastfeeding and metabolic stress can affect values in subadults.

Therefore, a human isotope difference is not automatically a direct dietary difference.

Exercise 4 — Read a boxplot

For Figure 3, identify:

the median;
the IQR;
the direction of any asymmetry;
any points beyond the whiskers;
which site appears more variable for each isotope; and
whether the site distributions overlap.

Then compare those visual impressions with Table 2.

Core: Perkins — Statistical concepts
Perkins’s Chapter 3 introduces the Week 4 vocabulary in a form designed for beginning social-science students (Perkins 2026).
Focus on:

NOIR measurement scales;
mean;
median;
mode;
trimmed mean;
MAD;
minimum and maximum;
range;
variance;
standard deviation;
skewness; and
kurtosis.

Pay particular attention to the worked standard-deviation example: the value of SD comes from distances around the mean, while squaring and square-rooting solve the cancellation and unit problems.

Core: Perkins — Descriptive statistics in R
Perkins’s Chapter 4 moves from concepts to computational practice (Perkins 2026).
Useful topics include:

calculating descriptives overall and by category;
comparing grouped distributions;
histograms;
faceting;
skewness and kurtosis; and
writing concise descriptive results.

Perkins uses psych::describe() and psych::describeBy(). In ANTH418, Assignment 1 uses an explicit dplyr::group_by() + summarise() workflow because seeing each calculated statistic directly makes the analytical logic more transparent.

Carlson — Looking at archaeological data
Carlson’s archaeological R chapter begins with the structure of archaeological datasets and the unit of observation (Carlson 2017).
For Week 4, concentrate on:

rows as observations;
columns as measurements;
missing values and NA;
looking at data before analysis;
numerical summaries;
normal and non-normal distributions; and
R’s handling and display of numerical values.

Carlson’s advice that the first step is to look at the data is the practical foundation of Assignment 1.

Banning — Descriptive statistics in archaeology
Banning’s chapter on summarizing archaeological data is especially valuable because it connects the formulas to archaeological measurement problems (Banning 2020).
Focus on:

mean as a center of gravity;
mean sensitivity to extremes;
trimmed means;
median and mode;
range;
IQR;
variance and SD;
CV;
skewness and kurtosis;
when means become misleading; and
categorical summaries.

Banning’s discussion of minimum-size thresholds for artifacts is a reminder that measurement protocols can change the mean before any statistical calculation begins.

Dressler — Thinking statistically about center and spread
Dressler’s first two chapters make central tendency and dispersion intuitive using a health-locus-of-control distribution (Dressler 2015).
Useful ideas include:

the mean as a model or educated guess;
how mean, median, and mode relate to distribution shape;
why skew separates the three centers;
deviations from the mean;
variance and SD;
the empirical 68–95 pattern; and
standardized scores.

One terminology caution for ANTH418: intervals such as mean ±1 SD describe the spread of observations under a normal model; do not call them confidence intervals for the population mean.

Privitera — Frequency, center, and variability
Privitera’s chapters separate the same descriptive workflow into three layers (Privitera 2024):

frequency distributions and graphs;
central tendency; and
variability.

Particularly useful Week 4 ideas include:

why summarization reveals patterns hidden in raw lists;
the mean as a balance point;
weighted means for unequal group sizes;
quartiles and IQR;
sample versus population variance;
standard deviation; and
the empirical rule.

Weighted means are useful background but are not required for Assignment 1, because the assignment describes each site separately rather than collapsing the two site means into one overall mean.

Plavcan — Why relative variation matters
Plavcan’s analysis of size variation in early Homo demonstrates that variability itself can be the anthropological phenomenon (Plavcan 2012).
Focus on:

multiple possible sources of biological size variation;
the dependence of absolute SD on overall body size;
CV as relative variability;
trait-specific comparative baselines; and
caution in using fossil variability to infer sex, taxonomy, or behavior.

The important lesson is not a universal CV cutoff. It is that dispersion becomes interpretable only relative to a defensible comparative framework.

Knipper et al. — Assignment 1 archaeological context
Read Knipper et al. not simply to find the “answer” but to understand why the assignment separates description from explanation (Knipper et al. 2020).
Focus on:

the El Argar archaeological setting;
La Bastida and Gatas;
human, animal, and cereal isotope sampling;
trophic-chain logic;
environmental influences on isotope values;
manuring and animal-feeding effects;
age-related isotope effects; and
the conclusion that higher human values at La Bastida do not necessarily mean different average dietary composition.







NoteAbout the uploaded Llull 2020 file



The file supplied under the name “Llull 2020 Protoapennine vs. Subapennine…” contains the Knipper et al. (2020) La Bastida–Gatas article rather than a distinct Llull ceramic study.
For that reason, this Study Guide does not attribute any independent argument to that mislabeled attachment. If the intended Llull article is supplied later, its ceramic case study can be added to this section.

Learning objectives
By the end of Week 2, you should be able to:

distinguish R, RStudio, and Quarto;
use an RStudio Project and project-relative file paths rather than machine-specific paths;
create and inspect basic R objects, vectors, and data frames;
recognize common data types, including numeric, character, logical, and missing (NA) values;
explain the basic syntax of an R function and use R’s help system;
import a CSV file with read_csv() and inspect it with glimpse(), names(), and dim();
use the native R pipe |&gt; to build readable sequences of data operations;
use introductory dplyr verbs to select, filter, transform, group, and summarize data;
construct a simple ggplot2 visualization;
create named Quarto code cells, use basic #| cell options, and render a .qmd file; and
re-render a document from a clean R session.

The Console shows + instead of &gt;
R thinks your command is incomplete—often because of a missing parenthesis, quotation mark, or bracket. Thulin uses this as an example of how R signals an unfinished expression (Thulin 2025).
Press Esc to cancel, then inspect the command.

Quarto for Scientists
Use Tierney’s Quarto for Scientists as a practical reference throughout the term (Tierney 2025). For Week 2, the most useful sections are:

workflow and RStudio Projects;
file paths and project organization;
the anatomy of a Quarto document;
code-cell labels and #| options;
keyboard shortcuts; and
figures, captions, citations, and cross-references.

The goal is not to learn every Quarto option now. Learn enough to build a document that you can reliably render again next week.

Course Data and Reproducibility
All ten graded assignments use real data derived from recent published archaeological or anthropological research. The original data are publicly available online through a journal supplement, Zenodo, GitHub, or another research repository.
For teaching purposes, the instructor may distribute a frozen, documented teaching file derived from the published source dataset. Such files may:

retain only variables needed for the assignment;
standardize column names or file format;
preserve source row identifiers;
remove blank spreadsheet rows or source-identified invalid observations; and
restrict the analysis to a clearly documented subset when the full dataset is unnecessarily large or complex.

Teaching files will not contain fabricated observations. Each assignment dataset will be accompanied by a short provenance note identifying the publication, original data source, variables retained, and any transformations or exclusions made before distribution.
Some original publications use advanced methods such as Bayesian modeling, PCA, machine learning, mixed-effects models, or multivariate procedures that lie beyond ANTH418. Students are not expected to reproduce those methods. Instead, assignments use a clearly stated ANTH418-level question that can be answered with techniques taught in the course while preserving the original observations and archaeological or anthropological context.

Learning objectives
By the end of Week 3, you should be able to:

distinguish an abstract concept, conceptual definition, operational definition, variable, indicator, and empirical value;
distinguish unidimensional from multidimensional variables and explain why multidimensional constructs usually require multiple indicators;
explain how theory assigns the roles of independent and dependent variables;
classify variables as nominal, ordinal, interval, or ratio, and explain what information each level preserves;
explain why collecting information at a higher defensible level of measurement preserves analytical options;
distinguish reliability, precision, accuracy, and validity;
describe interobserver/intercoder, test–retest, parallel-form, and internal-consistency approaches to reliability;
distinguish face, content, construct, and criterion validity;
explain why reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity;
evaluate whether a research question is feasible in relation to evidence, time, resources, access, and ethics;
classify social-science variables as internal states, external states, reported behavior, observed behavior, or artifacts/environment;
separate data type from analysis method when thinking about qualitative and quantitative research;
describe theoretical drive, pacing, and point of interface in mixed-methods research;
distinguish exploratory from confirmatory designs while recognizing that real research often moves between them;
state the four conditions normally required for a causal argument: covariation, nonspuriousness, temporal precedence, and mechanism;
identify the unit of analysis and explain the ecological fallacy;
classify a study using the three design decisions: observational/experimental, cross-sectional/longitudinal, and single-case/comparative;
explain the logic of QUAL → QUAN and QUAN → QUAL sequential designs;
explain how participatory and action research redistribute decisions about questions, methods, and outcomes; and
connect measurement and design to archaeological data provenance, reproducibility, metadata, uncertainty, and problem-driven methodological pluralism.

Concepts, constructs, indicators, and values
Some variables are straightforward. Others are constructs: complex concepts whose empirical meaning must be represented through several indicators.
Bernard and Wutich emphasize that even familiar concepts can become difficult when researchers ask exactly what is being measured (Bernard and Wutich 2027).
Consider “social status” in archaeology. We cannot directly ask a past individual about status. Possible indicators might include:

grave treatment;
access to rare materials;
house size;
spatial location;
diet;
craft specialization;
labor investment; or
association with particular objects.

None of these indicators is automatically equivalent to “status.” Each requires a theoretical argument linking the observable trace to the concept.
Banning describes this problem as indirect measurement: archaeologists often measure a surviving property as a proxy for something that cannot be observed directly, such as ancient population size, household wealth, social status, interaction, diet, or rainfall (Banning 2020).






WarningA proxy is an argument, not a fact



If grave wealth is used as a proxy for social status, the archaeological measurement of grave goods may be precise while the inference about status remains uncertain.
Measurement quality therefore has at least two levels:

Did we measure the observable evidence well?
Does that evidence validly represent the concept we claim it represents?

Strong design is alignment
Research design is not just the choice of a statistical test.
It is the architecture that connects:

the research question;
theoretical expectations;
units of analysis;
sampling;
variables and operational definitions;
data collection;
measurement quality;
analysis;
ethics;
feasibility; and
the conclusions the evidence can support.

Bernard and Wutich define research design as knowing how to answer a research question and test a hypothesis by coordinating sampling, data collection, and analysis (Bernard and Wutich 2027).
Banning similarly emphasizes that archaeological research design should reduce the possibility that apparent patterns are products of sampling problems, investigator bias, or measurement error (Banning 2020).

What is actually being analyzed?
The unit of analysis is the entity about which the study draws conclusions.
Examples include:

individual;
household;
grave;
artifact;
assemblage;
neighborhood;
settlement;
region; or
nation.

The unit of observation and unit of analysis are sometimes the same, but not always.
For example, measurements may be taken on individual artifacts but summarized to make an inference about assemblages.

Participatory research
Participatory research shifts the relationship from studying people as subjects toward building inquiry with participants.
Participants may help shape:

questions;
definitions;
recruitment;
data-collection tools;
interpretation; and
dissemination.

Bernard and Wutich emphasize that participatory approaches can make methods more culturally, socially, and ethically appropriate (Bernard and Wutich 2027).

Direct and indirect measurement
Banning distinguishes relatively direct measurements—such as length or mass—from indirect measures and proxies used to estimate phenomena that cannot be observed directly (Banning 2020).
Archaeology is full of indirect measurement.
Researchers may want to estimate:

ancient population;
household wealth;
interaction;
traffic in exchanged materials;
dietary contribution;
mobility;
social position; or
environmental conditions.

The evidence used for those inferences may be measurable, but the link between evidence and target concept remains theoretical.
That is why the Week 3 chain matters:

measure an archaeological property well → justify the proxy relation → choose a design that supports the inference

Data are produced, not simply found
Reiter and colleagues emphasize that archaeological data contain choices made during collection, classification, storage, harmonization, and interpretation (Reiter et al. 2024).
“Raw” data are therefore not neutral in the sense of being untouched by research practice.
Good data publication should preserve:

provenance;
original context;
identifiers;
measurement units;
methods;
laboratory metadata;
chronological associations;
category definitions; and
enough granularity for later reuse.

The BIAD Standards are motivated by a practical problem: data that lack metadata or are aggregated too early may become impossible to integrate or reinterpret later (Reiter et al. 2024).

Exercise 1 — From concept to variable

Choose one concept:

social inequality;
mobility;
ritual investment;
household wealth;
political participation; or
cultural identity.

Write:

a one-sentence conceptual definition;
one or more operational definitions;
the empirical values that would be recorded;
the likely level of measurement; and
one threat to validity.








TipWhat a strong answer should contain





A strong answer keeps the stages separate.
For example, for mobility you might distinguish a broad conceptual definition from a specific operational measure such as isotope-based classification, residential histories, GPS traces, or documented changes of residence.
Different operational definitions may produce different variables and answer different versions of the research question.

Exercise 2 — Classify levels of measurement

Classify each variable as nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio, or “identifier/not a measurement”:

archaeological site name;
preservation rank: poor / fair / good;
calibrated temperature in °C;
artifact mass in grams;
number of grave goods;
specimen ID;
Likert response from strongly disagree to strongly agree;
d13c value.








TipCheck your reasoning





A defensible classification is:

site name — nominal;
preservation rank — ordinal;
Celsius — interval;
mass — ratio;
count — ratio;
specimen ID — identifier, not a quantitative measurement;
Likert item — ordinal unless a stronger scale assumption is justified;
d13c — interval-like measurement.

The important part is the explanation, not merely the label.

Exercise 7 — Audit an archaeological variable

Select one variable used in archaeological research—for example:

“elite burial”;
“household wealth”;
“local/nonlocal”;
“craft specialization”;
“site size”;
“ceramic standardization”; or
“mobility.”

Write a short measurement audit:

conceptual definition;
operational definition;
unit of analysis;
measurement level;
likely reliability problem;
likely validity problem;
metadata required for reuse;
whether the measure is direct or proxy-based; and
what uncertainty should be reported.

Core: Bernard and Wutich — Chapters 1–3
The first three chapters of Social Research Methods provide the conceptual spine for Week 3 (Bernard and Wutich 2027).

Chapter 1 — Foundations of Social Research
Concentrate on:

variables;
simple versus multidimensional variables;
independent and dependent variables;
concepts and indicators;
conceptual and operational definitions;
levels of measurement;
reliability;
precision;
accuracy; and
validity.

Pay particular attention to the Chicano high-school example because it shows how a flawed operational definition can produce a misleading social explanation.


Chapter 2 — Preparing for Research
Focus on:

the difference between the ideal and real research process;
feasibility;
testability;
time, money, personnel, and access;
ethics;
the generation of research questions; and
the five broad classes of social variables.

The main lesson is that research questions, resources, ethics, and methods constrain one another from the beginning.


Chapter 3 — Research Design
Focus on:

exploratory and confirmatory research;
qualitative and quantitative data/analysis;
causal inference;
units of analysis;
the ecological fallacy;
observational versus experimental design;
cross-sectional versus longitudinal design;
single-case versus comparative design;
mixed methods; and
participatory/action research.

Archaeological measurement: Banning
Banning’s The Archaeologist’s Laboratory is particularly useful for translating social-science measurement language into archaeological practice (Banning 2020).
Focus on:

measurement scales;
precision;
reliability;
bias;
classification error;
validity;
quality assurance;
direct versus indirect measurement; and
research design and sampling.

Banning’s proxy examples are especially important: archaeology often measures surviving material properties in order to infer social or behavioral quantities that cannot be directly observed.

Mixed methods: Pelto
Pelto’s Mixed Methods in Ethnographic Research provides a historical and anthropological perspective on the combination of methods (Pelto 2017).
Use it to think beyond a simple qualitative-versus-quantitative divide.
Particularly useful themes include:

the long history of methodological mixing in ethnography;
QUAL + quan and QUAL → quan notation;
theoretical drive;
simultaneous versus sequential designs;
triangulation; and
the use of multiple methods for descriptive expansion rather than merely confirmation.

Research as a craft: Booth and colleagues
The Craft of Research is useful for thinking about research as an iterative intellectual process rather than a mechanical sequence (Booth et al. 2024).
For Week 3, focus on:

moving from topic to question;
turning a question into a research problem;
asking “So what?”;
recognizing that real research zigzags and doubles back; and
designing inquiry for an audience that will ask whether the evidence really supports the claim.

Archaeological data publication: Reiter and colleagues
The BIAD Standards show why research design continues into data publication (Reiter et al. 2024).
Key Week 3 connections include:

metadata;
explicit provenance;
traceable identifiers;
preserving original chronological associations;
publishing raw and grouped data where possible;
consistent vocabularies; and
designing data so that later researchers can reuse and integrate them.

1. What You Should Understand
By the end of Week 5, you should be able to:

explain EDA as an iterative mode of inquiry;
distinguish description from inference;
choose graphics from variable type and analytical purpose;
create and interpret bar charts, histograms, boxplots, scatterplots, and facets;
explain what a graphic preserves and hides;
explain the ggplot2 grammar: data, mappings, layers, geoms, stats, positions, scales, coordinates, facets, and themes;
distinguish mapping inside aes() from setting outside aes();
combine raw observations and summaries with layers;
recognize misleading graphical choices;
visualize sample structure before substantive patterns; and
recognize phylogenetic and spatial non-independence.

23. Practice Progression: What You Should Be Able to Do Without Copying
Use this as a self-check before beginning Assignment 2.

Level 1 — Read
Given a ggplot2 command, identify:

the dataset;
the variables mapped inside aes();
the geom;
fixed aesthetic settings;
labels and theme.



Level 2 — Modify
Given a working graph, change one purposeful element:

reorder categories;
change histogram bin width;
add raw observations to a boxplot;
change from one-panel grouping to facets;
improve labels.



Level 3 — Construct
Given a question and variables, choose and construct an appropriate graph without being told the geom.
Example:

“Are languages evenly represented across macroareas?”

You should recognize this as a categorical frequency question and choose a bar chart.


Level 4 — Interpret
Explain:

what the graph shows in the observed sample;
what graphical choices influence the display;
what the graph may hide;
what question it suggests next; and
what inferential conclusion it does not establish.

Assignment 2 mainly assesses Levels 3 and 4. The earlier practices are designed to get you there.

What You Should Understand
By the end of Week 7, you should be able to:

explain probability as a long-run relative frequency;
distinguish population/sample and parameter/statistic;
explain a sampling frame and why it matters;
distinguish probability from nonprobability sampling;
